# Mobius' round-robin database

## Watch the resolutions fill

The RRD is **four ring buffers**, one per resolution. One snapshot per second, each landing in exactly one buffer. Each scrape carries one **global value** (the second it happened), stored in whichever buffer takes it — so the same value is the same colour in *every* ring. The **dark** slice is the newest (write head); the readout calls out oldest→latest. Spin the clock: **seconds** fills in ~2 min, **minutes** ticks each minute, **hours** / **days** only on bigger jumps.

```elixir
# Four resolutions, each a ring buffer: {key, label, capacity, period, coarser_period}
# — the Mobius.RRD defaults; `coarser` is the period that "steals" this one's boundary.
# Plain data, no module: Nerves Livebook shares one BEAM across notebooks.
resolutions = [
  {:second, "seconds", 120, 1, 60},
  {:minute, "minutes", 120, 60, 3600},
  {:hour, "hours", 48, 3600, 86_400},
  {:day, "days", 60, 86_400, nil}
]

# Buttons that advance the simulated clock: {tag, label, seconds}.
rrd_steps = [
  {:s1, "+1s", 1},
  {:s10, "+10s", 10},
  {:m1, "+1m", 60},
  {:m10, "+10m", 600},
  {:h1, "+1h", 3600},
  {:h10, "+10h", 36_000},
  {:d1, "+1d", 86_400},
  {:d10, "+10d", 864_000}
]

rrd_buttons = Enum.map(rrd_steps, fn {tag, label, _secs} -> {tag, Kino.Control.button(label)} end)
rrd_reset = Kino.Control.button("Reset")
rrd_status = Kino.Frame.new()

# One global value per scrape: the second it happened. A scrape lands in exactly one
# buffer (the coarsest boundary it crossed) and that buffer stores this global value —
# so the same value has the same colour in every ring.
#
# `retained` gives the last `capacity` values a buffer holds: boundaries of `period`
# that aren't also a coarser boundary, newest-first.
retained = fn now, period, capacity, coarser ->
  div(now, period)
  |> Stream.unfold(fn
    0 -> nil
    k -> {period * k, k - 1}
  end)
  |> Stream.filter(fn t -> coarser == nil or rem(t, coarser) != 0 end)
  |> Enum.take(capacity)
end

# Hue from a value. Cycle length 997 is coprime to 60/3600/86400, so even the coarse
# rings (whose values jump by 3600/86400) get distinct hues instead of all-one-colour.
hue = fn value -> trunc(rem(value, 997) * 360 / 997) end

# A ring's slots. The k-th insert sits at slot rem(k-1, capacity); we map the last
# `capacity` retained values onto their slots and colour by global value. Newest (the
# write head) is drawn dark.
ring_data = fn now, period, capacity, coarser ->
  count = if coarser, do: div(now, period) - div(now, coarser), else: div(now, period)
  newest_slot = if count > 0, do: rem(count - 1, capacity), else: nil

  slot_value =
    now
    |> retained.(period, capacity, coarser)
    |> Enum.with_index()
    |> Map.new(fn {value, i} -> {rem(count - i - 1, capacity), value} end)

  for slot <- 0..(capacity - 1) do
    value = slot_value[slot]

    color =
      cond do
        slot == newest_slot -> "#111827"
        value != nil -> "hsl(#{hue.(value)}, 70%, 55%)"
        true -> "#eef0f3"
      end

    %{"slot" => slot, "color" => color, "w" => 1}
  end
end

# One small persistent donut per resolution. We push fresh data into each on every
# click with window: capacity, an in-place update that never flickers.
rrd_charts =
  Map.new(resolutions, fn {key, label, capacity, _p, _c} ->
    chart =
      VegaLite.new(width: 260, height: 260, title: "#{label} (#{capacity})")
      |> VegaLite.data(name: "ring")
      |> VegaLite.mark(:arc, inner_radius: 60, stroke: "white", stroke_width: 1, tooltip: true)
      |> VegaLite.encode_field(:theta, "w", type: :quantitative, stack: true)
      |> VegaLite.encode_field(:order, "slot", type: :quantitative)
      # scale: nil → the field values are used as literal CSS colours.
      |> VegaLite.encode_field(:color, "color", type: :nominal, scale: nil, legend: nil)
      |> Kino.VegaLite.new()

    {key, chart}
  end)

fmt_elapsed = fn now ->
  pad = fn n -> String.pad_leading(to_string(n), 2, "0") end
  "#{div(now, 86_400)}d #{pad.(rem(div(now, 3600), 24))}:#{pad.(rem(div(now, 60), 60))}:#{pad.(rem(now, 60))}"
end

render_rrd = fn now ->
  Enum.each(resolutions, fn {key, _label, capacity, period, coarser} ->
    Kino.VegaLite.push_many(rrd_charts[key], ring_data.(now, period, capacity, coarser), dataset: "ring", window: capacity)
  end)

  # Callouts: oldest → latest global value held in each ring.
  summary =
    Enum.map_join(resolutions, " · ", fn {_key, label, capacity, period, coarser} ->
      case retained.(now, period, capacity, coarser) do
        [] -> "#{label} –"
        vals -> "#{label} #{List.last(vals)}→#{List.first(vals)}"
      end
    end)

  Kino.Frame.render(rrd_status, Kino.Markdown.new("**Simulated uptime: #{fmt_elapsed.(now)}** — #{summary}"))
end

render_rrd.(0)

step_seconds = Map.new(rrd_steps, fn {tag, _label, secs} -> {tag, secs} end)

Kino.listen(
  Kino.Control.tagged_stream(rrd_buttons ++ [{:reset, rrd_reset}]),
  0,
  fn
    {:reset, _event}, _now ->
      render_rrd.(0)
      {:cont, 0}

    {tag, _event}, now ->
      now = now + Map.fetch!(step_seconds, tag)
      render_rrd.(now)
      {:cont, now}
  end
)

Kino.Layout.grid(
  [
    Kino.Layout.grid(Enum.map(rrd_buttons, fn {_tag, btn} -> btn end) ++ [rrd_reset], columns: 9),
    rrd_status,
    Kino.Layout.grid(Enum.map(resolutions, fn {key, _l, _c, _p, _co} -> rrd_charts[key] end), columns: 2)
  ],
  boxed: true
)
```

## One scrape, one buffer

Each scrape is stored **once**, in the coarsest buffer whose boundary it crossed. The window auto-zooms from 60 s up to 3 d as the run grows — watch each buffer settle into its own span: **seconds** ~2 min, **minutes** ~2 h, **hours** ~2 d, **days** beyond. Dense recent, sparse old, bounded.

```elixir
window_days = 3
span = window_days * 86_400

sample_steps = [
  {:s10, "+10s", 10},
  {:m1, "+1m", 60},
  {:m10, "+10m", 600},
  {:h1, "+1h", 3600},
  {:h10, "+10h", 36_000},
  {:d1, "+1d", 86_400}
]

sample_buttons = Enum.map(sample_steps, fn {tag, label, _s} -> {tag, Kino.Control.button(label)} end)
sample_reset = Kino.Control.button("Reset")
sample_status = Kino.Frame.new()

# {lane, period, capacity, coarser_period}: the four buffers and the period that
# "steals" their boundary (days has none).
lanes = [
  {"seconds", 1, 120, 60},
  {"minutes", 60, 120, 3600},
  {"hours", 3600, 48, 86_400},
  {"days", 86_400, 60, nil}
]

lane_res = %{"seconds" => "second", "minutes" => "minute", "hours" => "hour", "days" => "day"}

# The last `capacity` scrapes routed to a buffer: boundaries of `period` that aren't
# also a coarser boundary. Lazy + descending, so it stops after `capacity`.
retained = fn now, period, capacity, coarser ->
  div(now, period)
  |> Stream.unfold(fn
    0 -> nil
    k -> {period * k, k - 1}
  end)
  |> Stream.filter(fn t -> coarser == nil or rem(t, coarser) != 0 end)
  |> Enum.take(capacity)
end

humanize = fn s ->
  cond do
    s >= 86_400 -> "#{Float.round(s / 86_400, 1)} d"
    s >= 3600 -> "#{Float.round(s / 3600, 1)} h"
    s >= 60 -> "#{Float.round(s / 60, 1)} min"
    true -> "#{round(s)} s"
  end
end

# Every retained scrape within the last `visible` seconds, placed by age in its lane.
sample_points = fn now, visible ->
  for {lane, period, capacity, coarser} <- lanes,
      t <- retained.(now, period, capacity, coarser),
      now - t <= visible do
    %{"ago" => t - now, "lane" => lane, "res" => lane_res[lane]}
  end
end

# No fixed x domain: VegaLite refits the axis to the data on each push, so the window
# auto-zooms as the run grows. Labels hidden because the unit shifts from s to days.
sample_chart =
  VegaLite.new(width: 720, height: 170, title: "Retained scrapes — each stored in exactly one buffer")
  |> VegaLite.data(name: "samples")
  |> VegaLite.mark(:tick, thickness: 2, opacity: 0.85)
  |> VegaLite.encode_field(:x, "ago", type: :quantitative, axis: [title: "← older          now →", labels: false, ticks: false])
  |> VegaLite.encode_field(:y, "lane",
    type: :nominal,
    scale: [domain: ["seconds", "minutes", "hours", "days"]],
    axis: [title: nil]
  )
  |> VegaLite.encode_field(:color, "res",
    type: :nominal,
    scale: [
      domain: ["second", "minute", "hour", "day", "frame"],
      range: ["#9ca3af", "#60a5fa", "#f59e0b", "#ef4444", "transparent"]
    ],
    legend: nil
  )
  |> Kino.VegaLite.new()

# Visible window grows with the run: at least 60 s, at most `span` (3 d). Two invisible
# "frame" anchors at the window edges pin the axis to that span even when empty.
render_samples = fn now ->
  visible = now |> max(60) |> min(span)

  frame = [
    %{"ago" => 0, "lane" => "seconds", "res" => "frame"},
    %{"ago" => -visible, "lane" => "seconds", "res" => "frame"}
  ]

  Kino.VegaLite.clear(sample_chart, dataset: "samples")
  Kino.VegaLite.push_many(sample_chart, frame ++ sample_points.(now, visible), dataset: "samples")

  counts =
    Enum.map_join(lanes, " · ", fn {lane, period, capacity, coarser} ->
      n = retained.(now, period, capacity, coarser) |> Enum.count(fn t -> now - t <= visible end)
      "#{lane} #{n}"
    end)

  Kino.Frame.render(sample_status, Kino.Markdown.new("**Uptime #{humanize.(now)}** · window #{humanize.(visible)} — #{counts}"))
end

render_samples.(0)

sample_step_seconds = Map.new(sample_steps, fn {tag, _l, s} -> {tag, s} end)

Kino.listen(
  Kino.Control.tagged_stream(sample_buttons ++ [{:reset, sample_reset}]),
  0,
  fn
    {:reset, _event}, _now ->
      render_samples.(0)
      {:cont, 0}

    {tag, _event}, now ->
      now = now + Map.fetch!(sample_step_seconds, tag)
      render_samples.(now)
      {:cont, now}
  end
)

Kino.Layout.grid(
  [
    Kino.Layout.grid(Enum.map(sample_buttons, fn {_t, b} -> b end) ++ [sample_reset], columns: 7),
    sample_status,
    sample_chart
  ],
  boxed: true
)
```

## The real thing: live CPU at every resolution

The same four rings, driven by the **real CPU metric** Mobius stores — pre-filled from the persisted history, refreshed every second, hue mapped 0–100 % (green → red). Press **Do math** to spin up RSA key pairs on every core and watch it redline.

```elixir
cpu_metric = "system.cpu.utilization.percent"

cpu_rings = [
  {:second, "seconds", 120, 1},
  {:minute, "minutes", 120, 60},
  {:hour, "hours", 48, 3600},
  {:day, "days", 60, 86_400}
]

cpu_instance =
  Process.registered()
  |> Enum.find_value(:mobius, fn name ->
    case Atom.to_string(name) do
      "Elixir.Mobius.Scraper." <> i -> String.to_atom(i)
      _ -> nil
    end
  end)

# Inverted hue: 0 % → 120 (green), 100 % → 0 (red).
cpu_hue = fn pct -> round(120 * (100 - max(0.0, min(100.0, pct))) / 100) end

# One ring's slots from the pulled history, on a true time axis: one slot per
# period bucket, the last slot being the newest stored point's bucket, walking back
# one period per slot. Buckets with no stored data stay gray.
cpu_ring_data = fn points, newest_ts, period, capacity ->
  buckets =
    points
    |> Enum.group_by(fn %{timestamp: ts} -> div(ts, period) end, fn %{value: v} -> v end)
    |> Map.new(fn {bucket, vals} -> {bucket, Enum.sum(vals) / length(vals)} end)

  latest = div(newest_ts, period)

  for slot <- 0..(capacity - 1) do
    value = buckets[latest - (capacity - 1 - slot)]
    color = if value, do: "hsl(#{cpu_hue.(value)}, 75%, 50%)", else: "#eef0f3"
    %{"slot" => slot, "color" => color, "w" => 1}
  end
end

cpu_status = Kino.Frame.new()
cpu_math = Kino.Control.button("Do math")

cpu_charts =
  Map.new(cpu_rings, fn {key, label, capacity, _period} ->
    chart =
      VegaLite.new(width: 260, height: 260, title: "#{label} (#{capacity})")
      |> VegaLite.data(name: "ring")
      |> VegaLite.mark(:arc, inner_radius: 60, stroke: "white", stroke_width: 1, tooltip: true)
      |> VegaLite.encode_field(:theta, "w", type: :quantitative, stack: true)
      |> VegaLite.encode_field(:order, "slot", type: :quantitative)
      |> VegaLite.encode_field(:color, "color", type: :nominal, scale: nil, legend: nil)
      |> Kino.VegaLite.new()

    {key, chart}
  end)

# Pull the whole stored history once per refresh; every ring is derived from it.
refresh_cpu = fn ->
  points =
    cpu_metric
    |> Mobius.Charts.series({:summary, :average}, %{}, last: {60, :day}, mobius_instance: cpu_instance)
    |> Map.fetch!(:points)

  newest = List.last(points)
  newest_ts = (newest && newest.timestamp) || 0

  Enum.each(cpu_rings, fn {key, _label, capacity, period} ->
    Kino.VegaLite.push_many(cpu_charts[key], cpu_ring_data.(points, newest_ts, period, capacity), dataset: "ring", window: capacity)
  end)

  # The indicator shows the raw reported value (the last_value the poller emitted),
  # not a windowed average from the history.
  raw =
    cpu_metric
    |> Mobius.Charts.series(:last_value, %{}, last: {1, :minute}, mobius_instance: cpu_instance)
    |> Map.fetch!(:points)
    |> List.last()

  now_pct = if raw, do: "#{Float.round(raw.value * 1.0, 1)} %", else: "—"
  Kino.Frame.render(cpu_status, Kino.Markdown.new("**CPU now: #{now_pct}** — green 0 % → red 100 %"))
end

refresh_cpu.()

# Refresh every second.
Kino.listen(1000, fn _i -> refresh_cpu.() end)

# Light up the cores: 10k tasks of ECC keypair generation across every scheduler.
# Each task is ~5 ms (50 × P-256 keygen) — heavy enough to saturate, fine-grained
# enough that the schedulers keep the sampling and UI running.
Kino.listen(cpu_math, fn _event ->
  cores = System.schedulers_online()

  spawn(fn ->
    1..10_000
    |> Task.async_stream(
      fn _ -> for _ <- 1..50, do: :public_key.generate_key({:namedCurve, :secp256r1}) end,
      max_concurrency: cores,
      ordered: false,
      timeout: :infinity
    )
    |> Stream.run()
  end)
end)

Kino.Layout.grid(
  [
    cpu_status,
    cpu_math,
    Kino.Layout.grid(Enum.map(cpu_rings, fn {key, _l, _c, _p} -> cpu_charts[key] end), columns: 2)
  ],
  boxed: true
)
```

## Introduction

`Mobius.RRD` is four `CircularBuffer`s at different resolutions — fine detail recent, coarse detail older, all in constant memory:

| Resolution | Capacity | Covers |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `:second` | 120 | ~2 minutes |
| `:minute` | 120 | ~2 hours |
| `:hour` | 48 | 2 days |
| `:day` | 60 | ~2 months |

Building one by hand below; nothing here touches the live instance.

## Build an empty RRD

```elixir
alias Mobius.RRD

rrd = RRD.new()
```

Helper to count each resolution's buffer:

```elixir
peek = fn rrd ->
  for resolution <- [:second, :minute, :hour, :day] do
    buffer = Map.fetch!(rrd, resolution)

    %{
      resolution: resolution,
      stored: Enum.count(buffer),
      capacity: Map.from_struct(buffer).max_size
    }
  end
  |> Kino.DataTable.new()
end

peek.(rrd)
```

## Insert a run of snapshots

Simulate the scraper: one snapshot per second for 130 seconds, starting on a day boundary.

```elixir
base = 86_400 * 20_000

rrd =
  Enum.reduce(0..129, RRD.new(), fn offset, rrd ->
    ts = base + offset
    snapshot = [{"demo.metric", :last_value, offset, %{}}]
    RRD.insert(rrd, ts, snapshot)
  end)

peek.(rrd)
```

Counts: `:second` capped at 120 and evicting, `:minute` one per minute boundary, `:day` one (the first snapshot), `:hour` empty (130 s never crossed an hour).

## How the routing works

`RRD.insert/3` checks boundaries coarsest-first and stores in the first match:

* new **day**? → `:day` (reset lower boundaries)
* else new **hour**? → `:hour`
* else new **minute**? → `:minute`
* else new **second**? → `:second`
* else duplicate within the second → drop

One resolution per snapshot. Recent dense, old sparse, memory flat.

## Reading history back

`RRD.all/1` returns every snapshot as `{timestamp, snapshot}`, oldest→newest.

```elixir
all = RRD.all(rrd)
%{total_points: length(all), oldest: List.first(all), newest: List.last(all)}
```

`RRD.query/2,3` pull a time window — what `Mobius.Exports.plot/4` uses under the hood.

```elixir
# Everything from 30 seconds into the run onward
RRD.query(rrd, base + 100) |> length()
```

## Putting it together

1. Telemetry handler writes the value into ETS.
2. Once a second the scraper snapshots ETS → `RRD.insert/3`.
3. RRD files it into the right `CircularBuffer`, ageing and evicting.
4. Plot/export reads back with `RRD.query/3`.

Four fixed buffers → two months of history, no unbounded growth. Next: **Mobius histograms with DDSketch** for *distributions* under the same constraint.
